<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anguilla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anguilla is a low coral island in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territory</a>. English settlers arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> in 1650, and in 1882 it was joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a> in a single colony. Resenting rule from Saint Kitts, Anguillans rebelled in 1967 and declared independence; after British intervention in 1969 it became a separate dependency again, formally from 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anguilla.jpg" alt="Anguilla" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This work created by the United Kingdom Government is in the public domain. This is because it is one of the following … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Anguilla.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Anguilla lies east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and the Virgin Islands and directly north of Saint Martin, from which it is separated by the Anguilla Channel. The main island is about 16 miles (26 km) long and no more than about 3 miles (5 km) wide at its widest point, and the territory also includes smaller islands and cays with no permanent population, among them Dog Island, Scrub Island and Sombrero. It is flat and low-lying, built of coral and limestone, with thin, poor soil. The Italian word anguilla, meaning eel, gave the island its name, in reference to its shape. The Indigenous Arawakan name was Malliouhana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Indigenous peoples who migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> first settled Anguilla, and the earliest artefacts found there date from around 1300 BCE. Settlement remains date from 600 CE, and the rock ledges of Big Spring hold more than 100 petroglyphs from between 600 and 1200 CE. It is uncertain when Europeans first saw the island. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> built a fort in 1631, which the Spanish destroyed in 1633.</p><p>According to traditional accounts, English settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> colonised the island from 1650, planting tobacco and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. The French took it temporarily in 1666 and returned it the next year under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>. Enslaved Africans were probably brought by the early settlers, and archival evidence shows at least 100 enslaved people there by 1683. They worked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations, and in time they and their descendants vastly outnumbered the white settlers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in 1807 and outlawed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1834.</p><p>Anguilla was placed under the administrative control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> in 1825 and federated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a> in 1882, against the wishes of many Anguillans. Full adult suffrage came in 1952. In 1967 the associated state of Saint Kitts-Nevis-Anguilla gained internal autonomy, and on 30 May 1967 Anguillans forcibly ejected the Saint Kitts police and declared independence from Saint Kitts. The revolution, led by Atlin Harrigan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-webster">Ronald Webster</a> among others, aimed at separation and a return to British colonial status. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> sent 300 troops in March 1969, and the Anguilla Act 1971 confirmed British authority. Anguilla formally seceded from Saint Kitts and Nevis in 1980.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Anguilla is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territory</a> with The Valley as its capital. It is internally self-governing, with a governor appointed by the British government. Its people are mostly descended from enslaved Africans. English is the main language, alongside an English-lexifier Creole called Anguillian. Boat racing is the national sport.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anguilla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nevis lost its separate assembly and in 1882 was joined with Saint Kitts and <b>Anguilla</b> in the colony of Saint Christopher-Nevis-Anguilla, the forerunner of today's Saint Kitts and Nevis.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-nevis">Colony of Nevis</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1882 Saint Christopher was joined with Nevis and <b>Anguilla</b> as Saint Christopher-Nevis-Anguilla.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Colony of Saint Christopher</a></li><li><blockquote>The group comprised Antigua, Saint Kitts, Nevis and Montserrat, later joined by Barbuda, <b>Anguilla</b> and the British Virgin Islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/leeward-islands-colony">Leeward Islands colony</a></li><li><blockquote>Negotiations with Britain produced an interim arrangement, but when it broke down <b>Anguilla</b> declared itself a republic in February 1969 and expelled a British envoy.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-anguilla">Republic of Anguilla</a></li><li><blockquote>To cut administrative costs after the decline of sugar, Britain merged the separate presidencies of Saint Kitts and Nevis, with <b>Anguilla</b>, into a single colony in 1882, governed from Basseterre within the Leeward Islands federal colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/saint-christopher-nevis-anguilla">Saint Christopher-Nevis-Anguilla</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Anguilla's</b> separation, the remaining part of Saint Christopher-Nevis-Anguilla became independent on 19 September 1983 as the Federation of Saint Christopher and Nevis, with Kennedy Simmonds as prime minister and the British monarch as head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a></li></ul></section>
